1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:09,277
Herbert and I went on from bad to worse, in the way of increasing our debts,

2
00:00:09,277 --> 00:00:15,199
looking into our affairs, leaving Margins, and the like exemplary transactions;

3
00:00:15,199 --> 00:00:21,639
and Time went on, whether or no, as he has a way of doing; and I came of age,—in

4
00:00:21,641 --> 00:00:27,160
fulfilment of Herbert’s prediction, that I should do so before I knew where I was.

5
00:00:27,160 --> 00:00:29,839
Herbert himself had come of age eight months before me.

6
00:00:30,718 --> 00:00:35,438
As he had nothing else than his majority to come into, the event did not make a profound

7
00:00:35,438 --> 00:00:41,319
sensation in Barnard’s Inn. But we had looked forward to my one-and-twentieth birthday,

8
00:00:41,319 --> 00:00:45,718
with a crowd of speculations and anticipations, for we had both

9
00:00:45,718 --> 00:00:51,116
considered that my guardian could hardly help saying something definite on that occasion.

10
00:00:51,116 --> 00:00:54,116
I had taken care to have it well understood in Little Britain when

11
00:00:54,116 --> 00:00:59,961
my birthday was. On the day before it, I received an official note from Wemmick,

12
00:00:59,961 --> 00:01:04,438
informing me that Mr. Jaggers would be glad if I would call upon him at five in the afternoon

13
00:01:04,438 --> 00:01:09,800
of the auspicious day. This convinced us that something great was to happen, and

14
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:16,519
threw me into an unusual flutter when I repaired to my guardian’s office, a model of punctuality.

15
00:01:16,519 --> 00:01:20,839
In the outer office Wemmick offered me his congratulations, and incidentally rubbed

16
00:01:20,839 --> 00:01:25,680
the side of his nose with a folded piece of tissue-paper that I liked the look of. But he

17
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:29,839
said nothing respecting it, and motioned me with a nod into my guardian’s room.

18
00:01:31,597 --> 00:01:36,116
It was November, and my guardian was standing before his fire leaning

19
00:01:36,116 --> 00:01:41,758
his back against the chimney-piece, with his hands under his coattails.

20
00:01:41,758 --> 00:01:48,277
“Well, Pip,” said he, “I must call you Mr. Pip to-day. Congratulations, Mr. Pip.”

21
00:01:48,277 --> 00:01:54,000
We shook hands,—he was always a remarkably short shaker,—and I thanked him.

22
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:57,438
“Take a chair, Mr. Pip,” said my guardian.

23
00:01:57,438 --> 00:02:02,718
As I sat down, and he preserved his attitude and bent his brows at his boots,

24
00:02:02,718 --> 00:02:07,160
I felt at a disadvantage, which reminded me of that old time when I had been put

25
00:02:07,160 --> 00:02:13,199
upon a tombstone. The two ghastly casts on the shelf were not far from him, and their

26
00:02:13,199 --> 00:02:19,319
expression was as if they were making a stupid apoplectic attempt to attend to the conversation.

27
00:02:19,319 --> 00:02:22,237
“Now my young friend,” my guardian began,

28
00:02:22,237 --> 00:02:27,758
as if I were a witness in the box, “I am going to have a word or two with you.”

29
00:02:27,758 --> 00:02:30,078
“If you please, sir.”

30
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:36,277
“What do you suppose,” said Mr. Jaggers, bending forward to look at the ground, and then throwing

31
00:02:36,277 --> 00:02:42,519
his head back to look at the ceiling,—“what do you suppose you are living at the rate of?”

32
00:02:42,519 --> 00:02:44,839
“At the rate of, sir?”

33
00:02:44,839 --> 00:02:52,000
“At,” repeated Mr. Jaggers, still looking at the ceiling, “the—rate—of?” And then looked all round

34
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:57,559
the room, and paused with his pocket-handkerchief in his hand, half-way to his nose.

35
00:02:57,559 --> 00:03:01,519
I had looked into my affairs so often, that I had thoroughly destroyed any

36
00:03:01,519 --> 00:03:07,000
slight notion I might ever have had of their bearings. Reluctantly, I confessed

37
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:12,839
myself quite unable to answer the question. This reply seemed agreeable to Mr. Jaggers,

38
00:03:12,839 --> 00:03:18,961
who said, “I thought so!” and blew his nose with an air of satisfaction.

39
00:03:18,961 --> 00:03:25,680
“Now, I have asked you a question, my friend,” said Mr. Jaggers. “Have you anything to ask me?”

40
00:03:25,680 --> 00:03:30,000
“Of course it would be a great relief to me to ask you several questions, sir;

41
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,879
but I remember your prohibition.”

42
00:03:32,879 --> 00:03:36,078
“Ask one,” said Mr. Jaggers.

43
00:03:36,078 --> 00:03:39,199
“Is my benefactor to be made known to me to-day?”

44
00:03:39,199 --> 00:03:41,718
“No. Ask another.”

45
00:03:41,718 --> 00:03:44,961
“Is that confidence to be imparted to me soon?”

46
00:03:44,961 --> 00:03:49,917
“Waive that, a moment,” said Mr. Jaggers, “and ask another.”

47
00:03:49,917 --> 00:03:54,718
I looked about me, but there appeared to be now no possible escape from the inquiry,

48
00:03:54,718 --> 00:03:59,641
“Have-I—anything to receive, sir?” On that, Mr. Jaggers said, triumphantly,

49
00:04:00,879 --> 00:04:03,438
“I thought we should come to it!” and called to Wemmick

50
00:04:03,438 --> 00:04:10,519
to give him that piece of paper. Wemmick appeared, handed it in, and disappeared.

51
00:04:10,519 --> 00:04:17,038
“Now, Mr. Pip,” said Mr. Jaggers, “attend, if you please. You have been drawing pretty freely here;

52
00:04:17,038 --> 00:04:23,038
your name occurs pretty often in Wemmick’s cash-book; but you are in debt, of course?”

53
00:04:23,038 --> 00:04:25,800
“I am afraid I must say yes, sir.”

54
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:29,199
“You know you must say yes; don’t you?” said Mr. Jaggers.

55
00:04:29,199 --> 00:04:29,699
“Yes, sir.”

56
00:04:30,319 --> 00:04:36,436
“I don’t ask you what you owe, because you don’t know; and if you did know, you wouldn’t tell me;

57
00:04:36,438 --> 00:04:41,800
you would say less. Yes, yes, my friend,” cried Mr. Jaggers, waving his forefinger to

58
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:46,839
stop me as I made a show of protesting: “it’s likely enough that you think you wouldn’t,

59
00:04:46,839 --> 00:04:51,839
but you would. You’ll excuse me, but I know better than you. Now, take this piece of paper

60
00:04:51,839 --> 00:04:57,800
in your hand. You have got it? Very good. Now, unfold it and tell me what it is.”

61
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:03,411
“This is a bank-note,” said I, “for five hundred pounds.”

62
00:05:03,411 --> 00:05:04,917
“That is a bank-note,” repeated Mr. Jaggers,

63
00:05:04,917 --> 00:05:11,559
“for five hundred pounds. And a very handsome sum of money too, I think. You consider it so?”

64
00:05:11,559 --> 00:05:13,800
“How could I do otherwise!”

65
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:16,917
“Ah! But answer the question,” said Mr. Jaggers.

66
00:05:16,917 --> 00:05:20,961
“Undoubtedly.” “You consider it,

67
00:05:20,961 --> 00:05:26,839
undoubtedly, a handsome sum of money. Now, that handsome sum of money, Pip,

68
00:05:26,839 --> 00:05:34,480
is your own. It is a present to you on this day, in earnest of your expectations. And at the rate

69
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:40,800
of that handsome sum of money per annum, and at no higher rate, you are to live until the donor

70
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:46,879
of the whole appears. That is to say, you will now take your money affairs entirely into your

71
00:05:46,879 --> 00:05:53,038
own hands, and you will draw from Wemmick one hundred and twenty-five pounds per quarter,

72
00:05:53,038 --> 00:05:57,358
until you are in communication with the fountain-head, and no longer with the mere

73
00:05:57,358 --> 00:06:06,199
agent. As I have told you before, I am the mere agent. I execute my instructions, and I am paid

74
00:06:06,199 --> 00:06:13,237
for doing so. I think them injudicious, but I am not paid for giving any opinion on their merits.”

75
00:06:13,237 --> 00:06:17,519
I was beginning to express my gratitude to my benefactor for the great liberality with

76
00:06:17,519 --> 00:06:23,800
which I was treated, when Mr. Jaggers stopped me. “I am not paid, Pip,” said he, coolly,

77
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:29,597
“to carry your words to any one;” and then gathered up his coat-tails, as he had gathered

78
00:06:29,597 --> 00:06:35,519
up the subject, and stood frowning at his boots as if he suspected them of designs against him.

79
00:06:35,519 --> 00:06:38,277
After a pause, I hinted,—

80
00:06:38,277 --> 00:06:41,078
“There was a question just now, Mr. Jaggers, which you desired

81
00:06:41,078 --> 00:06:46,800
me to waive for a moment. I hope I am doing nothing wrong in asking it again?”

82
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:49,277
“What is it?” said he.

83
00:06:49,277 --> 00:06:54,436
I might have known that he would never help me out; but it took me aback to have to shape the

84
00:06:54,438 --> 00:07:01,480
question afresh, as if it were quite new. “Is it likely,” I said, after hesitating, “that my

85
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:11,080
patron, the fountain-head you have spoken of, Mr. Jaggers, will soon—” there I delicately stopped.

86
00:07:11,080 --> 00:07:17,038
“Will soon what?” asked Mr. Jaggers. “That’s no question as it stands, you know.”

87
00:07:17,038 --> 00:07:19,559
“Will soon come to London,” said I,

88
00:07:19,559 --> 00:07:25,680
after casting about for a precise form of words, “or summon me anywhere else?”

89
00:07:25,680 --> 00:07:29,641
“Now, here,” replied Mr. Jaggers, fixing me for the first time

90
00:07:29,641 --> 00:07:33,597
with his dark deep-set eyes, “we must revert to the evening when

91
00:07:33,597 --> 00:07:39,078
we first encountered one another in your village. What did I tell you then, Pip?”

92
00:07:39,078 --> 00:07:44,718
“You told me, Mr. Jaggers, that it might be years hence when that person appeared.”

93
00:07:44,718 --> 00:07:49,116
“Just so,” said Mr. Jaggers, “that’s my answer.”

94
00:07:49,116 --> 00:07:54,116
As we looked full at one another, I felt my breath come quicker in my strong desire to get something

95
00:07:54,116 --> 00:08:00,718
out of him. And as I felt that it came quicker, and as I felt that he saw that it came quicker,

96
00:08:00,718 --> 00:08:05,519
I felt that I had less chance than ever of getting anything out of him.

97
00:08:05,519 --> 00:08:09,758
“Do you suppose it will still be years hence, Mr. Jaggers?”

98
00:08:09,758 --> 00:08:13,319
Mr. Jaggers shook his head,—not in negativing the question,

99
00:08:13,319 --> 00:08:18,199
but in altogether negativing the notion that he could anyhow be got to answer it,—and the

100
00:08:18,199 --> 00:08:23,678
two horrible casts of the twitched faces looked, when my eyes strayed up to them,

101
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:29,641
as if they had come to a crisis in their suspended attention, and were going to sneeze.

102
00:08:29,641 --> 00:08:35,199
“Come!” said Mr. Jaggers, warming the backs of his legs with the backs of his warmed hands,

103
00:08:35,199 --> 00:08:39,839
“I’ll be plain with you, my friend Pip. That’s a question I must not be asked.

104
00:08:39,839 --> 00:08:43,398
You’ll understand that better, when I tell you it’s a question that might compromise

105
00:08:43,398 --> 00:08:48,961
me. Come! I’ll go a little further with you; I’ll say something more.”

106
00:08:48,961 --> 00:08:51,879
He bent down so low to frown at his boots,

107
00:08:51,879 --> 00:08:56,917
that he was able to rub the calves of his legs in the pause he made.

108
00:08:56,917 --> 00:09:01,879
“When that person discloses,” said Mr. Jaggers, straightening himself,

109
00:09:01,879 --> 00:09:06,917
“you and that person will settle your own affairs. When that person discloses,

110
00:09:06,917 --> 00:09:12,438
my part in this business will cease and determine. When that person discloses,

111
00:09:12,438 --> 00:09:19,800
it will not be necessary for me to know anything about it. And that’s all I have got to say.”

112
00:09:19,800 --> 00:09:26,358
We looked at one another until I withdrew my eyes, and looked thoughtfully at the floor.

113
00:09:26,358 --> 00:09:31,917
From this last speech I derived the notion that Miss Havisham, for some reason or no reason,

114
00:09:31,917 --> 00:09:38,718
had not taken him into her confidence as to her designing me for Estella; that he resented this,

115
00:09:38,718 --> 00:09:44,519
and felt a jealousy about it; or that he really did object to that scheme,

116
00:09:44,519 --> 00:09:50,199
and would have nothing to do with it. When I raised my eyes again,

117
00:09:50,199 --> 00:09:56,557
I found that he had been shrewdly looking at me all the time, and was doing so still.

118
00:09:56,559 --> 00:10:02,718
“If that is all you have to say, sir,” I remarked, “there can be nothing left for me to say.”

119
00:10:02,718 --> 00:10:05,839
He nodded assent, and pulled out his thief-dreaded watch,

120
00:10:05,839 --> 00:10:10,358
and asked me where I was going to dine? I replied at my own chambers,

121
00:10:10,358 --> 00:10:16,398
with Herbert. As a necessary sequence, I asked him if he would favour us with his company,

122
00:10:16,398 --> 00:10:22,038
and he promptly accepted the invitation. But he insisted on walking home with me, in order that

123
00:10:22,038 --> 00:10:28,237
I might make no extra preparation for him, and first he had a letter or two to write, and (of

124
00:10:28,237 --> 00:10:35,557
course) had his hands to wash. So I said I would go into the outer office and talk to Wemmick.

125
00:10:35,559 --> 00:10:41,398
The fact was, that when the five hundred pounds had come into my pocket, a thought had come into

126
00:10:41,398 --> 00:10:47,199
my head which had been often there before; and it appeared to me that Wemmick was a good person to

127
00:10:47,199 --> 00:10:52,879
advise with concerning such thought. He had already locked up his safe,

128
00:10:52,879 --> 00:10:58,519
and made preparations for going home. He had left his desk, brought out his two greasy

129
00:10:58,519 --> 00:11:03,559
office candlesticks and stood them in line with the snuffers on a slab near the door,

130
00:11:03,559 --> 00:11:09,116
ready to be extinguished; he had raked his fire low, put his hat and great-coat ready, and

131
00:11:09,116 --> 00:11:15,839
was beating himself all over the chest with his safe-key, as an athletic exercise after business.

132
00:11:15,839 --> 00:11:23,237
“Mr. Wemmick,” said I, “I want to ask your opinion. I am very desirous to serve a friend.”

133
00:11:23,237 --> 00:11:26,879
Wemmick tightened his post-office and shook his head, as if his opinion were

134
00:11:26,879 --> 00:11:29,116
dead against any fatal weakness of that sort.

135
00:11:31,078 --> 00:11:37,917
“This friend,” I pursued, “is trying to get on in commercial life, but has no money, and finds it

136
00:11:37,917 --> 00:11:44,398
difficult and disheartening to make a beginning. Now I want somehow to help him to a beginning.”

137
00:11:44,398 --> 00:11:49,436
“With money down?” said Wemmick, in a tone drier than any sawdust.

138
00:11:49,438 --> 00:11:54,839
“With some money down,” I replied, for an uneasy remembrance shot across me of that symmetrical

139
00:11:54,839 --> 00:12:02,438
bundle of papers at home—“with some money down, and perhaps some anticipation of my expectations.”

140
00:12:02,438 --> 00:12:07,519
“Mr. Pip,” said Wemmick, “I should like just to run over with you on my fingers, if you please,

141
00:12:07,519 --> 00:12:13,680
the names of the various bridges up as high as Chelsea Reach. Let’s see; there’s London, one;

142
00:12:13,680 --> 00:12:19,078
Southwark, two; Blackfriars, three; Waterloo, four; Westminster, five;

143
00:12:19,078 --> 00:12:24,398
Vauxhall, six.” He had checked off each bridge in its turn, with the handle of his

144
00:12:24,398 --> 00:12:30,436
safe-key on the palm of his hand. “There’s as many as six, you see, to choose from.”

145
00:12:30,438 --> 00:12:32,680
“I don’t understand you,” said I.

146
00:12:32,680 --> 00:12:37,839
“Choose your bridge, Mr. Pip,” returned Wemmick, “and take a walk upon your bridge, and pitch your

147
00:12:37,839 --> 00:12:42,879
money into the Thames over the centre arch of your bridge, and you know the end of it. Serve

148
00:12:42,879 --> 00:12:49,917
a friend with it, and you may know the end of it too,—but it’s a less pleasant and profitable end.”

149
00:12:49,917 --> 00:12:55,160
I could have posted a newspaper in his mouth, he made it so wide after saying this.

150
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:57,879
“This is very discouraging,” said I.

151
00:12:57,879 --> 00:13:01,237
“Meant to be so,” said Wemmick.

152
00:13:01,237 --> 00:13:07,358
“Then is it your opinion,” I inquired, with some little indignation, “that a man should never—”

153
00:13:07,358 --> 00:13:11,838
“—Invest portable property in a friend?” said Wemmick. “Certainly

154
00:13:11,839 --> 00:13:16,718
he should not. Unless he wants to get rid of the friend,—and then it becomes

155
00:13:16,718 --> 00:13:21,358
a question how much portable property it may be worth to get rid of him.”

156
00:13:21,358 --> 00:13:25,800
“And that,” said I, “is your deliberate opinion, Mr. Wemmick?”

157
00:13:25,800 --> 00:13:29,038
“That,” he returned, “is my deliberate opinion in this office.”

158
00:13:29,961 --> 00:13:35,641
“Ah!” said I, pressing him, for I thought I saw him near a loophole here;

159
00:13:35,641 --> 00:13:39,116
“but would that be your opinion at Walworth?”

160
00:13:39,116 --> 00:13:46,358
“Mr. Pip,” he replied, with gravity, “Walworth is one place, and this office is another. Much as the

161
00:13:46,358 --> 00:13:53,078
Aged is one person, and Mr. Jaggers is another. They must not be confounded together. My Walworth

162
00:13:53,078 --> 00:13:58,961
sentiments must be taken at Walworth; none but my official sentiments can be taken in this office.”

163
00:14:00,116 --> 00:14:07,038
“Very well,” said I, much relieved, “then I shall look you up at Walworth, you may depend upon it.”

164
00:14:07,038 --> 00:14:14,000
“Mr. Pip,” he returned, “you will be welcome there, in a private and personal capacity.”

165
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:18,680
We had held this conversation in a low voice, well knowing my guardian’s ears

166
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:25,160
to be the sharpest of the sharp. As he now appeared in his doorway, towelling his hands,

167
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:31,839
Wemmick got on his great-coat and stood by to snuff out the candles. We all three went into the

168
00:14:31,839 --> 00:14:38,718
street together, and from the door-step Wemmick turned his way, and Mr. Jaggers and I turned ours.

169
00:14:38,718 --> 00:14:43,116
I could not help wishing more than once that evening, that Mr. Jaggers had had an Aged in

170
00:14:43,116 --> 00:14:50,237
Gerrard Street, or a Stinger, or a Something, or a Somebody, to unbend his brows a little.

171
00:14:50,237 --> 00:14:54,639
It was an uncomfortable consideration on a twenty-first birthday, that coming of

172
00:14:54,641 --> 00:14:59,277
age at all seemed hardly worth while in such a guarded and suspicious world as he made of

173
00:14:59,277 --> 00:15:05,756
it. He was a thousand times better informed and cleverer than Wemmick, and yet I would a thousand

174
00:15:05,758 --> 00:15:12,438
times rather have had Wemmick to dinner. And Mr. Jaggers made not me alone intensely melancholy,

175
00:15:12,438 --> 00:15:17,800
because, after he was gone, Herbert said of himself, with his eyes fixed on the fire,

176
00:15:17,800 --> 00:15:21,800
that he thought he must have committed a felony and forgotten the details of it,

177
00:15:21,800 --> 00:15:24,983
he felt so dejected and guilty. Chapter 37.
